Alright, I've been progressively polishing the game engine. So far I've added tone mapping, ensured the sun light has a target, exposed the sun object from the world setup to the game instance, and added a death flash overlay that toggles on death. The next visual polish item on the list is "sun-follow" – making the directional light (the sun) track the player so that shadows are correctly oriented everywhere, especially in large worlds where the player can wander far from the origin.

I need to modify the game loop where the camera updates each frame. Currently, in `js/engine/game.js`, there's a section that updates the camera orbit based on the controller's position:

```js
      // camera orbit
      const look = this.controller.pos.clone();
```

I want to insert logic before this that moves the sun to follow the player. The sun object is now stored as `this.sun` on the game instance (I added that earlier when I changed the destructuring of `setupScene` to include `sun`). I also need to update the sun's target position, which is `sun.target.position`, so the directional light points at the player.

I should set the sun's position to be offset from the player – something like `player.x + 60, player.y + 110, player.z + 40`. Those offsets were used earlier in the world setup for the initial sun placement, so reusing them makes sense. Then set `sun.target.position` to exactly the player's position.

I must ensure I place this code right before the existing camera orbit code, preserving the comment about camera orbit. I’ll add a comment to explain the new block: “sun follows the player so shadows work everywhere in big worlds”. That will make the code self‑documenting.

The edit operation should replace the exact old string (the comment and the line) with the new block that includes the sun‑following code plus the original comment and line. I’ll use a non‑replace‑all edit targeting the specific snippet:

old_string:
```
      // camera orbit
      const look = this.controller.pos.clone();
```

new_string:
```
      // sun follows the player so shadows work everywhere in big worlds
      this.sun.position.set(this.controller.pos.x + 60, this.controller.pos.y + 110, this.controller.pos.z + 40);
      this.sun.target.position.copy(this.controller.pos);

      // camera orbit
      const look = this.controller.pos.clone();
```

That should integrate the sun‑follow behavior cleanly. No other files need touching for this feature, as the sun object is already passed through and the target was added to the scene earlier. I'll now issue the edit tool call with these parameters.

